(N1) 'the moduli are |D| for class-number-one discriminants D':
   m = 7:  D = -m is a discriminant, h = 1;  h(-4m) = 1
   m = 8:  D = -m is a discriminant, h = 1;  h(-4m) = 2
   m = 9:  D = -m is NOT a discriminant;  h(-4m) = 2
   m = 12:  D = -m is a discriminant, h = 1;  h(-4m) = 2
   m = 14:  D = -m is NOT a discriminant;  h(-4m) = 4
   m = 27:  D = -m is a discriminant, h = 1;  h(-4m) = 3
   m = 28:  D = -m is a discriminant, h = 1;  h(-4m) = 2
   -> holds for 7, 8, 27 (and 12, 28), fails for 9 and 14 (the Bailey and Rogers moduli): no map, typed NUMEROLOGY.

(N2) (F, U, S) and the defect F - U - S of the moduli:
   7 = Mat([7, 1]):  (F,U,S) = [0, 0, 0],  defect 0
   8 = Mat([2, 3]):  (F,U,S) = [2, 1, 1],  defect 0
   9 = Mat([3, 2]):  (F,U,S) = [1, 1, 1],  defect -1
   12 = [2, 2; 3, 1]:  (F,U,S) = [4, 2, 3],  defect -1
   14 = [2, 1; 7, 1]:  (F,U,S) = [2, 2, 2],  defect -2
   27 = Mat([3, 3]):  (F,U,S) = [2, 1, 1],  defect 0
   60 = [2, 2; 3, 1; 5, 1]:  (F,U,S) = [10, 3, 7],  defect 0
   the asked shapes are realised as p^2 = 9 (Bailey), pq = 14 (Rogers), p^2q = 12 (the period of ss(p));
   the balanced shapes as p = 7 (Rogers-Selberg), p^3 = 8, 27 (Goellnitz-Gordon/Jackson-Slater, Dyson), p^2qr = 60.
   hostile: F(12) = phi(12) = 4, but F(N) != phi(N) at the other p^2 q values:
     N = 12: F = 4, phi = 4
     N = 18: F = 4, phi = 6
     N = 20: F = 4, phi = 8
     N = 28: F = 4, phi = 12
     N = 44: F = 4, phi = 20
     N = 45: F = 4, phi = 24
     N = 50: F = 4, phi = 20

(N3) 'p^2qr = 60 = |A_5| is the Rogers-Ramanujan icosahedral group, so F(60) counts its subgroups':
   subgroup orders of A_5: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 12, 60] (classical); proper nontrivial: 7 orders
   proper nontrivial divisors of 60: [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 12, 15, 20, 30] (F = 10): 15, 20, 30 are not subgroup orders
   |PSL_2(F_l)| = l(l^2-1)/gcd(2, l-1) for l = 2, 3, 5, 7, 11: [6, 12, 60, 168, 660] with defects [-2, -1, 0, 4, 3]
   (pq, p^2q, p^2qr at l = 2, 3, 5 only; genuine content: #ss(X(l)) = (p-1)|PSL_2(F_l)|/12, a mass-formula fact)

(N4) 'the supersingular period 12 = 2^2 * 3 has shape p^2 q for a structural reason':
   the correction ss(p) - (p-1)/12 = (1/4)(1-(-4/p)) + (1/3)(1-(-3/p)) has conductor lcm(4, 3) = 12,
   and 4 = |disc Z[i]|, 3 = |disc Z[(1+sqrt(-3))/2]| are the two CM discriminants with extra units:
   disc of Z[i] = -4, disc of Z[omega] = -3;  so 12 = 2^2 * 3 is |D_-4| |D_-3|.
   this explains the factorisation of 12, not any role of F(12), U(12), S(12).
